  "prompt_text": "The figure puts the lead content of a perovskite photovoltaic module in context by comparing it with the amount of metals already present in ordinary soil. A red slab at the top represents a perovskite PV module and is labeled with about 0.4 g m⁻² of Pb²⁺. Below it, a thin gray layer represents the top 1 cm of natural soil, which contains roughly 0.3–1.2 g m⁻² of lead. This visual comparison suggests that the total lead per unit area in a module is of the same order as the lead naturally found in a very shallow soil layer.\n\nA second soil block on the right gives typical concentration ranges for several metals in soil, including lead at about 10–50 mg kg⁻¹, cadmium below 1 mg kg⁻¹, and iron around 50–200 mg kg⁻¹. Dashed guide lines connect the module to the soil layers, reinforcing the area-based comparison.\n\nOverall, the graphic is a scale and context illustration rather than a process diagram: it emphasizes that the lead loading in a perovskite solar module appears comparable to background lead already present in natural soil over a small depth.",
